    "logo_url": "",
    "name": "Multivariable Association Analysis of Amplicon Data",
    "readme": "# Multivariable Association Analysis of Amplicon Data\n\nDownstream multivariable association analysis of amplicon sequencing data starting from an ASV/OTU phyloseq object. The workflow includes sequencing-control assessment, contaminant identification and removal based on negative control samples, visualization and assessment of positive control samples, batch-effect correction, normalization, feature filtering, and multivariable association and differential abundance analysis. Analyses can be performed at the ASV/OTU level or on features aggregated to different taxonomic levels. The workflow supports the incorporation of multiple fixed and random effects, continuous and categorical metadata variables, and relevant covariates to account for potential confounding factors and repeated or correlated measurements. Users can select appropriate normalization and transformation strategies and statistical models, enabling the identification of microbial features associated with biological or experimental variables of interest while accounting for complex study designs.\n\n## Main inputs\n\nThe workflow starts from a **phyloseq object** and a corresponding **sample metadata table**.\nThe metadata sample identifiers must match the sample names in the phyloseq object.\n\nThe workflow also asks for:\n\n- the metadata column identifying **negative sequencing controls**,\n- the metadata column identifying **positive sequencing controls**,\n- the metadata column containing the **batch ID**,\n- the **MaAsLin3 model formula**,\n- the **MaAsLin3 reference level**,\n- the **maximum significance threshold**,\n- the **normalization method**,\n- the **transformation**,\n- and the **multiple-testing correction method used to compute q-values**.\n\nThe MaAsLin3 formula should use metadata column names and should **not** include the leading `~`.\nThe same workflow-level MaAsLin3 settings are used for both the ASV/OTU-level and taxonomically aggregated analyses.\n\nFor formula syntax and additional MaAsLin3 examples, see the\n[MaAsLin3 tutorial](https://www.bioconductor.org/packages/release/bioc/vignettes/maaslin3/inst/doc/maaslin3_tutorial.html).\n\n## Trying additional taxonomic aggregation levels\n\nThe workflow contains one taxonomic agglomeration branch. To explore additional taxonomic levels without modifying the complete workflow:\n\n1. Run the workflow once to generate the **batch-corrected phyloseq object**.\n2. Open **Phyloseq Taxonomic Agglomeration** as an individual Galaxy tool.\n3. Select another taxonomic rank, for example Family or Genus.\n4. Run **MaAsLin3** on the resulting aggregated feature table using the workflow metadata and the desired model settings.\n5. If an additional taxonomic level should become part of the reproducible workflow, duplicate the `Taxonomic Agglomeration -> MaAsLin3` branch in the workflow editor and configure the desired rank explicitly.\n\nThis approach is useful for exploratory analyses while keeping the main workflow compact.\n\n## Trying alternative MaAsLin3 settings\n\nFor exploratory parameter testing, it is usually not necessary to rerun the complete workflow each time.\n\n1. Run the workflow once with a baseline set of parameters.\n2. Use the generated ASV/OTU-level or taxonomically aggregated feature table as input to the standalone **MaAsLin3** Galaxy tool.\n3. Try alternative normalization methods, transformations, significance thresholds, or q-value correction methods.\n4. Compare the resulting significant-feature tables and diagnostic/summary plots.\n5. Once an appropriate configuration has been selected, set these values through the workflow inputs and rerun the workflow for a reproducible final analysis.\n\nChanging normalization or transformation changes the values entering the statistical model, so alternative settings should be interpreted as distinct analysis configurations rather than cosmetic changes.\n\n## Main outputs\n\nKey outputs include the batch-corrected ASV table, the reconstructed batch-corrected phyloseq object,\ndifferential-abundance result tables, significant-feature tables, and diagnostic or differential-abundance plots.\n",
    "report": {
        "markdown": "# Multivariable Association Analysis of Amplicon Data\n\nThis report summarises the inputs, key outputs, and workflow structure for downstream\namplicon analysis including sequencing-control assessment, decontamination, batch-effect\ncorrection, phyloseq reconstruction, taxonomic aggregation, and multivariable differential\nabundance analysis.\n\n## Workflow Inputs\n```galaxy\ninvocation_inputs()\n```\n\n## Workflow Outputs\n```galaxy\ninvocation_outputs()\n```\n\n## Workflow\n```galaxy\nworkflow_display()\n```\n"
    },
